    <record id="channel_own_events_s1_l1" model="slide.slide">
        <field name="channel_id" ref="channel_own_events" />
        <field name="is_published" eval="True" />
        <field name="name">Fees and hours</field>
        <field name="slide_category">article</field>
        <field name="sequence">11</field>
        <field name="html_content" type="html"><div class="oe-course-content"><p>On your event's <span class="oe-path">Entertainment</span> tab, each performance in a stage's running order takes a <strong>Fee</strong>. Only confirmed performances with a fee are owed. Hired staff, like a bartender, are paid by the hour: give their shift an <strong>Hourly Rate</strong> (Logistics), and once they check out, their hours times the rate are owed.</p><p>The tab shows what's owed and what's billed, and <strong>Bill Performers &amp; Staff</strong> makes one draft vendor bill per person for the event, ready to check and post in Accounting.</p><div class="oe-diagram"><img src="/performers_elearning_events/static/img/event_payouts.png" alt="An event's Entertainment tab with what's owed" class="img-fluid" /><p class="oe-diagram-caption"><span class="oe-diagram-caption-tag">Screenshot</span>$550 owed, ready to bill.</p></div><div class="oe-callout"><span class="oe-callout-icon"><i class="fa fa-file-text-o" /></span><div><span class="oe-callout-label">1099-NEC</span><p>In the US, anyone billed this way who has no 1099 setting yet is marked as a 1099-NEC vendor, so their payments add up in the 1099 summary. Untick it on the contact for a corporation.</p></div></div><div class="oe-callout oe-callout-good"><span class="oe-callout-icon"><i class="fa fa-repeat" /></span><div><span class="oe-callout-label">Cancelled a bill?</span><p>A line whose bill was cancelled can be billed again.</p></div></div></div></field>
    </record>
